00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  when i was a little kid  i had a lot of nightmares which was  weird  because i had a very happy and healthy  and safe childhood  i had a loving family so it's just weird  being a kid is weird brains are weird  life is weird  whatever i had a lot of nightmares  and some of the bad ones rank among my  most vivid childhood memories  i had dreams about evil trees and dreams  about giant eels  it was about shape-shifting grandmas  yeah those stay with you over time  not being able to fall asleep because  you're afraid when you're a kid is  a very dark thing but the strange thing  is i really wouldn't change any of that  i actually kind of treasure it  that's because having nightmares as a  kid and spending time with that gave me  two  massive blessings and i'll share one now  and one at the end  the first is that i became very very  good at figuring out whether i was in a  dream or real life  and knowing the difference is everything  nightmares really tend to  lose their edge when you know they're  not real things chase you a little bit  slower  and scary things become smaller and the  world just gets a little more pliable  and weird things start to happen you can  do strange things  when you know it's not real this kind of  overwhelming euphoria  and a feeling of empowerment when i  figured out that i could fly in my  dreams  as a kid it was profoundly cathartic  it was healing because it allowed me to  take something which terrified me about  my brain and being alive and turned into  something  creative and joyful and actually  welcomed my nightmares because they  helped me figure out  that i was in a dream  and it wasn't until i turned 18 that i  realized this wasn't my private  mental invention to beat nightmares but  a phenomenon called lucid dreaming  and lucid dreaming is where you figure  out that you're in a dream and you start  to control it  you can do whatever you want you can you  can fly you can breathe underwater  you can move things with your mind you  can shoot ice out of your hands  there's really no known limits to what  you can do in a lucid dream  and when i got to university i stumbled  on the work of stephen laburge who's a  psychophysiology phd  from stanford who in the 80s developed  the foundational  literature on what lucid dreaming is and  how to do it  and i just fell all the way down this  rabbit hole i just devoured his work  and there was this online corpus of  lucid dreaming techniques put together  by a very  eccentric community of lucid dreamers on  how to do it and i wasn't the only one  studying this there were other students  on campus  who had mastered the art of lucid  dreaming flying was nothing to them they  were having out-of-body experiences  and composing music in their dreams and  raising landscapes with a swipe of their  hand  and the craziest thing that i learned  falling down that rabbit hole  is that the techniques on how to lucid  dream can be learned  practiced and mastered by anyone willing  to try  so i'm just going to walk you through  some of those techniques that i've found  incredibly useful  over the past few years  so there's three fundamental gates to  lucid dreaming and every night that you  don't have a lucid dream  you have failed to pass through one of  these three gates  they are in order realize did you  realize that you were in a dream  that's step one remain  once you realize that you're in a dream  were you able to stay there without  waking up  and then most importantly did you  remember that that dream happened at all  so i'll just start by talking about the  first one which is also my favorite  how do you have that epiphany where you  realize that you're in a dream  it's this electric moment and the only  way to  create that moment is to ask as often as  possible in reality  am i dreaming right now and expert lucid  dreamers are checking many times a day  whether they're in a dream or reality  so i'm just going to walk you through  some of those reality checks that you  can use to distinguish  number one and i hope people will do  this with me this is your north star for  freaking out if you're a dream  shut your nose with your hand and then  try and breathe through it  this should not be working  but if it is working congratulations  you're almost definitely dreaming right  now  this talk is about to get way more  interesting for you  but for the rest of us it's just  important to remember that  breathing is always regulated by what  you're doing in reality if your  sleeping body is breathing you're always  going to be able to breathe in a dream  this is a great way to distinguish  another easy one  is that you can take out your phone and  look at the home screen and look at the  time  and put it away and then look back at it  again if the numbers change  significantly  or if they don't look like numbers at  all you're very likely to be in a dream  that's just because brains have a hard  time holding alphanumeric symbols in  place  while you're in a dream they shift  around a lot  then there's this other one which is uh  kind of frightening honestly  look at your hands and just count the  fingers  if you have about 10 and they look like  hands  you're probably in reality but if not  you're almost definitely in a dream  this is very consistent i just have to  warn you once you realize you've never  had normal hands  in your dreams your entire life and  didn't notice it can kind of blow you  away  this one's also a bit more discreet than  like the nose thing so that's a personal  preference i'll let you decide  and then finally i had to include this  one because it's just too weird light  switches don't work in dreams  they just don't brains can't handle  changing the visual landscape of a space  that quickly  um and let's just admit that plenty of  light switches don't work in real life  so  you can get some false positives with  this  so just be careful with that okay  so once you've achieved uh lucidity and  you're having that moment  how do you avoid waking up immediately  this is actually a lot harder than it  sounds  people under appreciate this because  most lucid dreams will last only like  five or ten seconds which  is a bummer okay so this is a bit more  art than science but really important  you have to act super nonchalant as soon  as you realize you're in a dream  you just have to act like you don't care  at all and you're gonna get a rush of  adrenaline  and your heartbeat will go up so you  actually have to pretend like someone's  watching you  i have to do this every time um and the  point is that if you can get through  this 5 or 10 second window  of over excitement then you're probably  going to have the whole dream which is  great  and then more concretely you have to  focus on your body in the dream  not your body in real life so the way i  do that is in the dream i just rotate my  hands in little circles  and i just look at my body and um  the point is that you can't become aware  of your sleeping body in your bed if you  do that you're going to get ejected  out of your dream and thrown back into  that body um so that'll end it  immediately  okay so you've had a lucid dream how do  you actually remember that it happened  and this is so important and so  difficult because if you don't remember  it it's really as if it didn't happen at  all  and this is hard 95 to 99 of dreams are  totally forgotten  and many people report not having dreams  at all  which to me is as tragic as it is  totally misinformed because  they've shown in labs over and over that  everyone dreams every night it's just a  matter of remembering it  so many people know that dream journals  really help with this you can train that  muscle  using a dream journal but they just  don't seem to work for a lot of people  and the reason is that they're missing a  key aspect which is that the dream  journal has to be at  arm's length from where you're sleeping  it has to be under your pillow under  your mattress or on your bedside table  and the reason for that is that standing  up and moving around is what wipes your  dream memory completely  you have to practice remembering  everything from a lying down position  and if you don't have a dream journal  handy you can just use a smartphone and  take a voice memo too  any bullets or fragments you remember  help with this and it is a muscle that  you can train  over time and then much more bizarrely  and this has been shown to work by the  national sleep foundation  in empirical studies just say out loud i  want to remember my dreams before you go  to bed  this actually works and the basis for  this is in  uh the psychology dark magic of  cognitive dissonance and we definitely  don't have time to talk about that but  suffice to say this is easy and we  should all be doing it  okay so those are some of the important  techniques but why does this matter why  am i even talking about this  flying is cool we like flying  it's a proven nightmare therapy we like  that nightmares are bad  um but i think it's worth admitting that  no one really knows  why this is valuable or even why it's  possible like there's no scientific  consensus on why humans need to  sleep much less why they dream and lucid  dreaming is thought to be more a bug  than a feature of human development  so why is this worth our time and  attention  the second blessing that having  nightmares as a kid gave me was a truth  that i've always taken to be  self-evident which is that having a  healthy relationship with your mind  is everything that's everything that's  what life is life is whatever happens in  your brain before you die  and on one hand that's terrifying  because brains aren't always good  they'll sometimes do stuff like  give kids nightmares which is weird  and living life in the cage of your mind  is kind of a bummer  but over time in the process of  spiritual the the  process of lucid dreaming which i can  only describe as spiritual  um i've come to realize that turning  your mind into a creative space  where you can experiment and witness  yourself  and have that moment of awakening where  you're like oh these colors  and these faces and these landscapes  that's all me  and just witness the mystery of your  mind  that's among the greatest joys there is  in life  and people wake up from lucid dreams  excited to  listen to what their mind is telling  them and then witness that mystery  throughout their life  and understand their mind better so i  encourage all of you  just try it just play with it  it may change for you as it did for me  how you experience life in your own mind  thank you  you

